    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the total number of games played in the baseball league, we need to consider the structure of the games between the teams. Each team plays 3 games against each of the other 8 teams, and since each game involves two teams, we need to account for this counting of games multiple times.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the total number of matchups without considering the order of the teams (i.e., the number of different ways to choose 2 teams out of 9 to play a game). This can be calculated using the combination formula \\(\\binom{9}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{9}{2} = \\frac{9 \\times 8}{2 \\times 1} = 36\n\\]\n\nThis calculation represents the total number of unique pairs of teams that can play against each other. Since each pair of teams plays 3 games, the total number of games played is:\n\n\\[\n36 \\times 3 = 108\n\\]\n\nHowever, this counts every single game twice (once for each team in the matchup), because a game involves two teams. To find the total number of distinct games played, we need to divide this product by 2:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{108}{2} = 54\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of games played in the season is \\(\\boxed{54}\\).",
